Article 7 The chief accountant shall be responsible for organizing the following work of the unit:
(1) Preparing and implementing budgets, financial revenue and expenditure plans, and credit plans, drafting plans for fund raising and utilization, developing financial resources, and using funds effectively;
(2) Conducting cost and expense forecasting, planning, control, accounting, analysis and assessment, and supervising the relevant departments of the unit to reduce consumption, save expenses, and improve economic efficiency;
(3) Establishing and perfecting the economic accounting system, and using financial and accounting data to conduct economic activity analysis;
(4) Undertaking other tasks assigned by the principal administrative leader of the unit.

Article 16 The chief accountant must possess the following qualifications:
(1) Uphold the socialist orientation and actively serve socialist construction and reform and opening up;
(2) Adhere to principles, be honest and perform duties impartially;
(3) After obtaining the qualification as an accountant, have worked in financial accounting in charge of a unit or a major aspect of a unit for no less than three years;
(4) Possess a relatively high level of theory and policy, be familiar with the state's financial and economic laws, regulations, guidelines, policies, and systems, and master relevant knowledge of modern management;
(5) Possess basic professional knowledge of the industry, be familiar with the industry situation, and have strong organizational and leadership skills;
(6) Be in good health and capable of performing the duties of the position.

Article 17 Where a chief accountant achieves remarkable results in work and falls under any of the following circumstances, he or she shall be rewarded in accordance with the state's provisions on rewards and punishments for enterprise employees or staff members of state administrative organs:
(1) Achieving remarkable results in strengthening financial accounting management, applying modern accounting methods and technical means, and improving the level of financial management and economic benefits;
(2) Achieving remarkable results in organizing economic accounting, tapping the potential for increasing production and saving, increasing revenue and reducing expenditure, accelerating capital turnover, and improving the effectiveness of capital use;
(3) Making outstanding contributions in safeguarding the state's financial and economic discipline, resisting illegal acts, protecting state property, and preventing or avoiding major losses to state property;
(4) Having outstanding deeds in building a clean government;
(5) Having other outstanding achievements or exemplary deeds.

Article 18 Where a chief accountant falls under any of the following circumstances in work, he or she shall, depending on the severity of the circumstances, be given disciplinary sanctions in accordance with the state's provisions on rewards and punishments for enterprise employees or staff members of state administrative organs:
(1) Violating laws, regulations, guidelines, policies, or financial systems, causing serious disorder in accounting work;
(2) Failing to resist, stop, or report acts such as tax evasion or tax fraud, interception of revenues that should be turned over to the state, indiscriminate issuance of bonuses or subsidies, squandering or wasting state assets, or harming state interests, thereby causing losses to state interests;
(3) Committing serious errors within the scope of work under his or her charge, or causing losses to state interests due to dereliction of duty;
(4) Using power for personal gain, practicing fraud, or engaging in malpractice for personal benefit, thereby causing losses to state interests or producing adverse effects;
(5) Having other acts of dereliction of duty or serious mistakes.
If a chief accountant commits any of the acts listed in the preceding paragraph, and the circumstances are serious enough to constitute a crime, the judicial authority shall investigate criminal liability according to law.
